    "id": "dataset-141557",
    "name": "Alton Brown's Yeast Doughnuts",
    "category": "Breakfast",
    "time": "2 hr",
    "servings": 20,
    "summary": "Good old A.B. sure does know his way around the kitchen! Here's a great recipe from Good Eats I thought I'd post. We're making these right now and I cannot wait to eat some! You will need a kitchen scale for this, as you have to weigh the flour. Cooking tim...",
    "ingredients": [
      "vegetable shortening",
      "yeast",
      "warm water",
      "nutmeg",
      "all - purpose flour",
      "peanut oil vegetable oil"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "1 1/2 cups milk",
      "2 1/2 ounces vegetable shortening (approximately 1/3 cup)",
      "2 1/2 tablespoons instant yeast",
      "1/3 cup warm water (95 to 105 degrees F)",
      "2 eggs, beaten",
      "1/4 cup sugar",
      "1 1/2 teaspoons salt",
      "1 teaspoon nutmeg",
      "23 ounces all-purpose flour, plus more for dusting surface (this is by weight, not volume)",
      "peanut oil or vegetable oil, for frying"
    ],
    "instructions": "Place the milk in a medium saucepan and heat over medium heat just until warm enough to melt the shortening.\nPlace the shortening in a bowl and pour warmed milk over. Set aside.\nIn a small bowl, sprinkle the yeast over the warm water and let dissolve for 5 minutes.\nAfter 5 minutes, pour the yeast mixture into the large bowl of a stand mixer and add the milk and shortening mixture, first making sure the milk and shortening mixture has cooled to lukewarm.\nAdd the eggs, sugar, salt, nutmeg, and half of the flour.\nUsing the paddle attachment, combine the ingredients on low speed until flour is incorporated and then turn the speed up to medium and beat until well combined.\nAdd the remaining flour, combining on low speed at first, and then increase the speed to medium and beat well.\nChange to the dough hook attachment of the mixer and beat on medium speed until the dough pulls away from the bowl and becomes smooth, approximately 3 to 4 minutes.\nTransfer to a well-oiled bowl, cover, and let rise for 1 hour or until doubled in size.\nOn a well-floured surface, roll out dough to 3/8-inch thick. Cut out dough using a 2 1/2-inch doughnut cutter or pastry ring and using a 7/8-inch ring for the center whole.\nSet on floured baking sheet, cover lightly with a tea towel, and let rise for 30 minutes.\nPreheat the oil in a deep fryer or Dutch oven to 365 degrees F.\nGently place the doughnuts into the oil, 3 to 4 at a time. Cook for 1 minute per side.\nTransfer to a cooling rack placed in baking pan.\nAllow to cool for 15 to 20 minutes prior to glazing, if desired.",
