    "id": "dataset-44860",
    "name": "Glazed Doughnuts",
    "category": "Breakfast",
    "time": "60 min",
    "servings": 1,
    "summary": "This is the recipe my MIL made when I was a child. My SIL, who was a friend from my childhood as well, used to bring these to school for her birthday treats. Our classes always looked forward to 'her' birthday each year! One year I had the privlege of being...",
    "ingredients": [
      "milk",
      "yeast yeast",
      "lukewarm water",
      "flour"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "1 cup milk",
      "1 (1/4 ounce) package yeast or 2 1/2 teaspoons bulk yeast",
      "1 cup lukewarm water",
      "1/2 cup shortening",
      "2/3 cup sugar",
      "2 eggs, beaten",
      "1 teaspoon salt",
      "7 cups flour"
    ],
    "instructions": "Scald milk; cool to lukewarm.\nSoften yeast in warm water (and proof by allowing to set for 10 minutes, watching for bubbling to begin to occur); add to milk.\nCream the shortening and sugar together.\nAdd eggs and beat.\nAdd salt to milk and yeast mixture.\nAlternately add flour and creamed shortening, sugar and eggs mixture.\nStir until smooth.\nCover and let stand until double in bulk.\nRoll 1/2 inch thick on lightly floured surface.\nCut and let rise until double in bulk, again.\nFry in deep fat fryer until light brown on each side.\nRemove and place on paper towels to absorb excess oil (into the paper towels, rather than the doughnuts).\nChange paper towels frequently.\nWhile still warm: Glaze with powdered sugar glaze.\nIf you prefer, they can be put into a sack with dry powdered sugar, crystal sugar or brown sugar and shaken gently to coat, or frosted with one of your favorite icings and sprinkles or nuts.\nWhen completely cool and glaze or frosting has set, store in an airtight container.\nThey can also be frozen for later use (eliminate as much air as possible).\nIf frozen, when ready to serve, remove from freezer, place on microwave-safe dish, cover with wax paper and run on defrost cycle until warmed through.\nThey will be almost as fresh as when you first made them!",
