    "id": "dataset-327876",
    "name": "Halloween Waffles",
    "category": "Breakfast",
    "time": "2 hr",
    "servings": 1,
    "summary": "Dorie Greenspan; suggested toppings—maple syrup, homemade applesauce, pumpkin butter, or maple butter.",
    "ingredients": [
      "unsalted butter",
      "acorn squash",
      "mcintosh apple",
      "pecans chopped",
      "yellow cornmeal",
      "baking powder",
      "cinnamon",
      "cloves",
      "apple cider",
      "pure maple syrup"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "4 tablespoons unsalted butter",
      "6 ounces acorn squash, peeled and seeded",
      "1 medium mcintosh apple",
      "1/3 cup pecans, coarsely chopped",
      "1 1/4 cups all-purpose flour",
      "1/3 cup yellow cornmeal",
      "1 tablespoon double-acting baking powder",
      "1/8 teaspoon salt",
      "1/4 teaspoon ground cinnamon",
      "1/8 teaspoon ground cloves",
      "1 1/4 cups apple cider",
      "2 tablespoons pure maple syrup",
      "2 large eggs"
    ],
    "instructions": "Preheat waffle iron; if you want to hold the finished waffles until serving time, preheat your oven to 200°.\nMelt butter; reserve.\nUse the large-holed side of a box grater to grate the acorn squash; you should have 1 ¼ cups packed shredded squash.\nPlace the squash in a bowl; peel and core apple; cut it into small dice and add it to the bowl with the squash; add in pecans, stir to mix; set aside.\nIn a big bowl, whisk the flour, cornmeal, baking powder, salt, cinnamon, and cloves.\nIn another bowl, whisk the cider, syrup, and eggs together until very well mixed.\nPour the liquid ingredients over the dry ingredients and stir with a whisk until just combined.\nStir in the squash, apple, and pecan and fold in the melted butter.\nLightly butter or spray the grids of your waffle iron; brush or spray again only if subsequent waffles stick.\nSpoon out a scant 1 cup of batter onto the grids; use a metal spatula or wooden spoon to spread the chunky batter evenly across the grids.\nClose the lid and bake until golden.\nIf you are not serving the waffles immediately, put them in a single layer on a baking sheet, loosely covered with foil, in the preheated oven while you make the rest of the batter.",
