    "id": "dataset-354473",
    "name": "A Campfire You Can Eat",
    "category": "Lunch",
    "time": "15 min",
    "servings": 1,
    "summary": "This looks like so much fun. I can't wait to do it with my kids. From Family Fun Magazine.",
    "ingredients": [
      "flour tortillas",
      "licorice",
      "peanuts",
      "peanut butter",
      "fried Chinese noodles",
      "Tootsie Roll",
      "pretzel",
      "white grape juice",
      "hot cocoa powder",
      "corn"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "12 inches flour tortillas",
      "red licorice, rope",
      "peanuts",
      "peanut butter",
      "fried Chinese noodles",
      "Tootsie Roll",
      "mini pretzel stick",
      "white grape juice",
      "hot cocoa powder",
      "candy corn"
    ],
    "instructions": "To make an edible campfire, first clear a space on the table to build a safe fire.\nLay down a tortilla fire base and wrap a licorice rope safety circle around the tortilla about an inch in from the edge.\nBuild a peanut rock ring halfway between the safety circle and the center of the fire base.\nSpread a circle of peanut butter in the center of the fire base; lay a small handful of fried Chinese noodles on top for kindling.\nLay Tootsie Roll logs around the peanut butter circle.\nUse mini pretzel sticks as fuel wood to build a teepee inside the ring of logs and over the kindling, sticking the pretzels into the peanut butter at a 45-degree angle.\nAdd another layer of logs, setting them across the corners of the first layer to form a box around the teepee.\nLay a few more pieces of fuel wood across the logs.\nMake sure buckets of water (glasses of grape juice) and dirt (hot cocoa powder) are nearby to put out the fire if necessary.\nLight the fire by adding candy corn flames.\nAfter the camp director approves the fire, throw dirt on the fire to put it out.\nNow, the moment the fire builders have been waiting for: Eat your fires!",
