    "id": "dataset-111738",
    "name": "Tea Marbled Eggs",
    "category": "Lunch",
    "time": "45 min",
    "servings": 6,
    "summary": "Fun to make and a nice addition to a Chinese meal, not just for decoration and they are always a talking point. The flavour is despite the spices not very strong--delicate is a better word. I like to serve them with the Chinese porridge/soup Congee.",
    "ingredients": [
      "soy sauce",
      "five spice powder",
      "black tea leaves tea"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "6 -8 eggs",
      "water",
      "1/2 teaspoon salt",
      "3 tablespoons soy sauce",
      "1 -2 star anise",
      "1 teaspoon five-spice powder",
      "2 tablespoons black tea leaves, is preferred but your normal tea will work too"
    ],
    "instructions": "Boil the eggs in water for approx 8 minutes, depending on size.\nLift the eggs out and place in cold water.\nTo the water in the pot add the salt, soy, star anise, five spice powder and tea.\nWhen eggs are cool enough to handle, gently crack the shells with the back of a spoon until the entire shell is a network of cracks, do not peel!\nBring the water in the pot back to the boil, and return the eggs to the pot, they need to be covered with water, add more if needed.\nGently boil them for 30 minutes.\nSwitch off the heat and allow the eggs to cool in the liquid for the minimum of 3 hours.\nPeel and serve them cut in half or quarters as a snack or use them as a garnish.",
