    "summary": "Simple preparation, minimum of ingredients, but full of flavor. If you've been searching for a recipe in which to use your soybean paste, this is it!",
    "ingredients": [
      "pork tenderloin fat connective tissue",
      "mirin rice wine",
      "ginger",
      "clove garlic",
      "red bell pepper",
      "peanut oil",
      "sambal oelek chili paste",
      "soybean paste",
      "soy sauce"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "3/4 lb pork tenderloin (all fat and connective tissue removed)",
      "1 tablespoon mirin (rice wine)",
      "2 slices ginger",
      "1 clove garlic, paper removed,flattened but still in one piece",
      "1 leek",
      "1 red bell pepper",
      "2 tablespoons peanut oil",
      "1 tablespoon sambal oelek or 1 tablespoon chili paste, of your choice",
      "2 tablespoons soybean paste",
      "1 tablespoon dark soy sauce"
    ],
    "instructions": "Place the pork in a large saucepan and cover with water; bring to the boil.\nWhen it is boiling, add the mirin and ginger.\nCook the pork for 20 minutes, then remove from the water and allow to cool (discard the other contents of the pan).\nWhen the pork is cool enough to handle, slice across the grain as thinly as possible in pieces about 2 inches long.\nClean the leek and slice in half lengthwise, then slice in 1 inch pieces.\nClean the bell pepper, remove seeds, and chop in pieces similar in size to the leek.\nHeat a wok over medium-high to high heat.\nAdd the oil, and when it is hot, add the flattened garlic clove.\nFry the garlic until it is very brown, then remove it and discard.\nAdd the chopped leek to the wok, and cook for 1 minute, stirring all the while.\nAdd the bell pepper pieces, and cook for about 30 seconds, stirring continuously.\nPush the vegetables to the side of the wok and add the sambal oelek in the middle; heat briefly.\nAdd the soybean paste, soy sauce, and the pork slices, mixing all well and insuring the pork is covered with all the spicy mixture.\nCook only for another 1-2 minutes, until everything is heated through.",
    "photo": "",
    "recipeSource": "https://www.food.com/recipe/twice-cooked-pork-hui-guo-rou-111659",
