    "id": "dataset-33844",
    "name": "Chicken Chasseur",
    "category": "Dinner",
    "time": "2 hr",
    "servings": 1,
    "summary": "This is a nice elegant meal to serve. The flavor improves in the refrigerator. I use thigh meat because it has a rich flavor, and it stands up to the braising process. I also remove the skin, just because it looks better in the finished product. You may use...",
    "ingredients": [
      "skinless chicken breasts whole chicken",
      "virgin olive oil",
      "shallots",
      "button mushroom",
      "dry white wine",
      "tomato paste",
      "chicken stock",
      "chopped fresh tarragon dried tarragon",
      "chopped parsley"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "8 skinless chicken breasts or 1 whole chicken, cut into 8 pieces",
      "5 tablespoons extra virgin olive oil",
      "2 shallots, finely chopped",
      "1/4 lb button mushroom, sliced",
      "1 tablespoon all-purpose flour",
      "1/2 cup dry white wine",
      "2 tablespoons Bourbon or 2 tablespoons whiskey",
      "2 teaspoons tomato paste",
      "3/4 cup chicken stock",
      "2 teaspoons chopped fresh tarragon or 2 teaspoons dried tarragon",
      "1 teaspoon chopped parsley"
    ],
    "instructions": "Heat the oil to medium in a large skillet, or buffet casserole (it has to be able to hold the liquid and take a lid).\nSauté the chicken until golden brown on both sides.\nRemove to a plate or bowl and reserve.\nSauté the shallots until soft but not brown.\nAdd the mushrooms, put on the lid, and cook for 3 minutes.\nAdd the flour and cook, stirring, for 1 minute.\nAdd the white wine, stirring as you do, then the brandy, tomato paste, and chicken stock.\nBring to a simmer, stirring; then reduce the heat.\nAdd the tarragon.\nPut the chicken and juices back into the pan.\nCover and simmer for 45 to 60 minutes, or until the chicken is tender.\nServe directly from the buffet casserole, or remove to a platter.\nIf your parsley is not bitter tasting, sprinkle with chopped parsley.",
