    "id": "dataset-470529",
    "name": "Duck Breast With Pomegranate Glaze",
    "category": "Dinner",
    "time": "60 min",
    "servings": 4,
    "summary": "Don't be put off by all the steps, this is a pretty easy recipe. You can also use the glaze and sauce on a whole roast duck, instead of duck breasts.The duck breasts can be seared a day in advance; the pomegranate glaze can be prepared in advance as well; c...",
    "ingredients": [
      "pomegranate molasses",
      "red wine vinegar",
      "chicken stock chicken broth",
      "skin breasts",
      "salt",
      "ground black pepper",
      "unsalted butter",
      "pomegranate seeds"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "2/3 cup pomegranate molasses (see TIP)",
      "2/3 cup red wine vinegar",
      "1/4 cup honey",
      "2 cups chicken stock or 2 cups chicken broth, reduced to 1/2 cup",
      "4 boneless skin-on duck breasts (about 7 ounces each)",
      "kosher salt",
      "fresh ground black pepper",
      "1 tablespoon unsalted butter",
      "1/2 cup pomegranate seeds, for garnish"
    ],
    "instructions": "For the glaze and sauce:\nCombine the pomegranate molasses and vinegar in a small saucepan over high heat.\nBring to a boil, then reduce the heat to medium and add the honey; mix well.\nCook for 12 to 15 minutes, until the mixture has reduced by about half (about 2/3 cup).\nRemove from the heat.\nTransfer half of the glaze (1/3 cup) to a separate small saucepan over medium-low heat.\nAdd the reduced stock or broth and stir to combine; keep warm.\nFor the duck:\nHeat a large skillet over medium-high heat.\nUse a knife to lightly score the skin on the duck breasts in a crosshatch pattern; do not cut through to the flesh.\nSeason both sides of the breasts with salt and pepper to taste.\nPlace skin side down in the hot skillet. (You may need to use 2 pans or do this in batches.).\nCook for 7 to 8 minutes, until nicely browned, then turn the breasts over and cook for 3 to 4 minutes on the second side (for medium-rare).\nBrush the duck breasts generously on both sides with the reserved 1/3 cup of pomegranate molasses-red wine vinegar glaze (there may be glaze left over).\nTent loosely with aluminum foil and let rest for 5 minutes.\nFinishing the sauce:\nReturn the pomegranate glaze-stock/broth mixture to medium-low heat.\nWhen it is heated through, add the remaining tablespoon of butter, stirring to combine.\nCook for 3 to 4 minutes, until the sauce coats the back of a spoon.\nAdjust seasoning with salt and pepper, as needed.\nFinishing the dish:\nWhen ready to serve, cut the duck breasts into quarter-inch slices.\nFan the slices on individual plates and drizzle the sauce over the duck.\nIf desired, sprinkle with pomegranate seeds.\nServe hot or warm.",
