    "id": "dataset-186375",
    "name": "French Bread Pudding",
    "category": "Dessert",
    "time": "2 hr",
    "servings": 8,
    "summary": "This is a little labor intensive, but worth it. Serve with creme anglaise (I didn't post recipe for creme, since there are several fine versions already posted here). This recipe came to me from a friend's sister. NOTE: This is very sweet, so adjust amount...",
    "ingredients": [
      "dried apricots chopped",
      "dried currant dried cranberries",
      "orange liqueur",
      "day - old French bread crusts",
      "butter",
      "granulated sugar",
      "water",
      "tartar",
      "egg yolks",
      "heavy cream",
      "milk",
      "vanilla extract",
      "salt",
      "nutmeg"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "2 tablespoons dried apricots, chopped",
      "1 tablespoon dried currant (or dried cranberries, chopped)",
      "3 tablespoons orange liqueur",
      "6 cups day-old French bread, crusts removed, cut into 3/4-inch cubes",
      "1/4 cup butter, melted",
      "1 3/4 cups granulated sugar",
      "1/2 cup water",
      "1/4 cup water (very cold)",
      "1 pinch cream of tartar",
      "4 large eggs",
      "2 egg yolks",
      "1 cup heavy cream",
      "1 cup milk",
      "2 teaspoons vanilla extract",
      "1 pinch salt",
      "1/4 teaspoon ground nutmeg"
    ],
    "instructions": "Marinate dried fruit in orange liqueur for 1 hour.\nPlace bread in large bowl and drizzle with butter; toss to coat.\nMix 1 cup sugar, 1/2 cup water, and cream of tartar in heavy medium sauce pan. Cook over medium-high heat, stirring occasionally, until sugar is melted and amber colored, 10-12 minutes.\nCarefully add the 1/4 cup cold water and swirl to incorporate.\nQuickly pour half the mixture into a 1-quart ovenproof souffle dish or casserole, tilting to coat bottom and part way up sides.\nPreheat oven to 350°F.\nPress one-third of the bread mixture into souffle dish; sprinkle with one-third of the dried fruit mixture. Repeat twice.\nBeat 3/4 cup sugar with remaining ingredients in a large bowl; pour over the bread.\nDrizzle remaining caramelized sugar over top (if hardened, heat over low heat to liquify).\nSet souffle dish in a large, deep pan. Fill pan with hot water half way up sides of dish.\nBake on center rack until set and browned, 50-55 minutes.\nRemove souffle dish from pan to wire rack and let stand 30 minutes.\nDust with confectioners’ sugar; serve with creme anglaise.",
