    "id": "dataset-126425",
    "name": "Chinese Muslim Style Spiced Cold Beef",
    "category": "Snack",
    "time": "60 min",
    "servings": 8,
    "summary": "\"This delicious cold spiced beef was a re-creation of an old memory that haunted my family periodically\" after the restaurant where they had eaten it closed, writes Mai Leung in her book \"Dim Sum and Other Chinese Street Food.\" I haven't tried this but am p...",
    "ingredients": [
      "oil",
      "raw peanuts skins",
      "ground beef",
      "szechwan bean sauce",
      "cayenne pepper",
      "szechwan pepper",
      "minced garlic",
      "white vinegar",
      "black soy sauce",
      "sesame oil",
      "flank steaks tender beef",
      "celery",
      "red bell pepper",
      "watercress"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "1 cup oil",
      "1/4 cup raw peanuts, skins removed",
      "4 ounces ground beef",
      "2 tablespoons szechwan bean sauce (regular, not hot)",
      "1/2 teaspoon cayenne pepper",
      "1/2 teaspoon ground szechwan pepper",
      "3 tablespoons sugar",
      "1 tablespoon minced garlic",
      "3 tablespoons white vinegar",
      "3 tablespoons black soy sauce",
      "1 tablespoon sesame oil",
      "8 bay leaves",
      "1 1/2 lbs flank steaks or 1 1/2 lbs tender beef",
      "1 cup celery, in thin strips 1 . 5 inches long",
      "1/2 cup scallion, in thin strips 1 . 5 inches long",
      "1/4 cup red bell pepper, in strips",
      "1/2 cup watercress"
    ],
    "instructions": "Heat 1 cup oil in wok to deep-fry temperature, then deep-dry peanuts over medium-low heat until golden brown.\nRemove the peanuts with a slotted spoon or strainer and place on paper towels to drain and cool.\nPut the cooled peanuts in a small plastic bag or between two towels, then roll a rolling pin over them to crush coarsely; set crushed peanuts aside for later use.\nMix the cayenne, Szechwan peppercorn, sugar, garlic, vinegar, black soy sauce, and sesame oil in a bowl.\nRemove all but two tablespoons of oil from the wok. Heat this oil, and when it is hot, add the ground beef.\nStir and cook the beef until done.\nStir in the bean sauce, then the sauce mixture you have just made.\nMix well, then turn off the heat.\nThis meat sauce can be prepared a few hours in advance.\nFill a big pot with enough water to cover your piece of beef.\nAdd the bay leaves to the water and bring to a rapid boil.\nAdd meat and cook over medium heat until done to your taste - medium rare should be about 10 minutes.\nRemove meat and pat dry.\nPlace the meat on a cutting board and slice into thin pieces, as you would for roast beef.\nArrange the slices on a serving platter, slightly overlapping each other.\nGarnish with celery, scallions, red pepper, and watercress.\nPour the meat sauce over evenly, then sprinkle crushed peanuts on top.\nServe at room temperature, perhaps with noodles.",
