    "id": "dataset-136979",
    "name": "Chinese Pork Fried Rice",
    "category": "Pasta",
    "time": "30 min",
    "servings": 2,
    "summary": "Although others have said that their recipes for Chinese Fried Rice is authentic, that is really an impossibility, because different Chinese cooks prepare it slightly differently, so what is authentic? I have seen recipes that did not contain sesame oil, ye...",
    "ingredients": [
      "smoked pork chops",
      "rice",
      "onion",
      "mixed peas carrots",
      "soy sauce",
      "vegetable oil",
      "sesame oil"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "2 smoked pork chops",
      "2 cups instant rice",
      "3/4 medium onion (diced)",
      "1/2 cup mixed peas and carrots (frozen)",
      "1/4 cup soy sauce",
      "2 eggs",
      "2 tablespoons vegetable oil",
      "2 tablespoons sesame oil"
    ],
    "instructions": "In a small pot bring the water to a boil. Stir in the rice. Cover with a lid and set aside.\nCut the pork into tiny diced pieces.\nIn a large skillet, place the vegetable oil over medium-high heat and get it hot. Place onions into skillet and fry until almost transparent. Add the peas and carrots, stirring until thawed and heated thoroughly.\nThe rice should now be ready. Pour rice into skillet. Add the diced pork, soy sauce and sesame oil.\nIn a small bowl, with a fork, beat the eggs well. Slowly pour the beaten eggs into the rice mixture, constantly stirring with a fork in a back and forth action, rather than a circular action. You do not want the eggs to solidify like scrambled eggs or fried eggs.\nYou could omit the pork and have just Chinese Fried Rice.",
