    "id": "dataset-210487",
    "name": "Kung Pao Prawns (Shrimp)",
    "category": "Seafood",
    "time": "60 min",
    "servings": 4,
    "summary": "From Firehouse Food. If you don't like spicy food, you can reduce the amount of chilis and chili oil.",
    "ingredients": [
      "long white rice",
      "vegetable oil",
      "prawns",
      "garlic cloves minced",
      "dried red chilies arbol",
      "chili oil",
      "soy sauce",
      "white vinegar",
      "dried roasted peanuts",
      "carrot",
      "celery",
      "chicken broth",
      "sesame oil",
      "green onions pale parts"
    ],
    "ingredientDetails": [
      "2 cups long-grain white rice",
      "1 tablespoon vegetable oil",
      "2 lbs medium prawns, peeled and deveined",
      "2 garlic cloves, minced",
      "3 small dried red chilies, such as arbol",
      "1 -2 teaspoon chili oil",
      "2 tablespoons soy sauce",
      "1/2 teaspoon sugar",
      "1 teaspoon distilled white vinegar",
      "1/2 cup dried roasted peanuts",
      "1 medium carrot, peeled and thinly sliced on the diagonal",
      "1 stalk celery, thinly sliced on the diagonal",
      "1/2 cup chicken broth",
      "1 teaspoon cornstarch",
      "2 teaspoons sesame oil",
      "salt",
      "3 green onions, white and pale green parts only, thinly sliced on the diagonal, for garnish"
    ],
    "instructions": "Cook the rice according to the package directions and keep warm.\nHeat the vegetable oil over high heat in a wok or large, deep sided skillet. Add the prawns, garlic, and chiles; stir fry for 2 minutes. Stir in the chili oil, soy sauce, sugar, and vinegar. Add the peanuts, carrot, and celery. Stir to combine and reduce the heat to low. Cover and cook for 3 minutes.\nMeanwhile, whisk together the broth and cornstarch in a small bowl. Add the cornstarch mixture to the wok, stir to combine, and bring to a boil. Cook until the sauce thickens, 2 to 3 minutes. Stir in the sesame oil and season to taste with salt.\nGarnish with the green onions and serve with rice.",
